00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.750
Chris: Coding standards are critical, as when a
developer writes code, they need to make sure that any

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.840
other developer in the same team can understand it.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.450
A developer who is not consistent with their
coding items such as naming conventions,

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:19.870
will run into problems later down the line,
especially if they are working as part of a team.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:26.220
If each developer in a team is writing code in their
own style, then this will certainly cause issues.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:33.949
The consequences of not following coding standards can
compromise security and code performance issues and

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.869
would make it difficult to maintain or update the code.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:38.920
Leah: Security concerns

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:45.193
If code is inconsistent, contains bugs or
has logical errors, then the software will

00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:47.363
be more vulnerable to security attacks.

00:00:47.743 --> 00:00:52.663
This is especially the case with complex code [e.g.

00:00:52.663 --> 00:00:54.653
not DRY], and errors in the code.

00:00:55.273 --> 00:01:01.183
Maintaining ‘Coding standards’ can reduce code
complexity, the number of bugs, and improve the

00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:06.353
debugging process as they would help the team
to prevent errors or detect issues earlier.

00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:08.433
Chris: Performance issues

00:01:08.913 --> 00:01:13.673
Coding standards help reduce the performance
issues of the software that may arise due

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.983
to the result of poor coding practices.

00:01:16.483 --> 00:01:23.113
So, for example if a program was complex, it could take
longer to execute or perform in the way it was expected.

00:01:23.753 --> 00:01:28.653
This would not only have an impact on its
performance, but also on the client or customer

00:01:28.753 --> 00:01:31.663
or the end user’s confidence and expectations.

00:01:32.643 --> 00:01:35.383
Leah: Difficulty in maintaining or updating the code

00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:40.633
If there are no standards and each developer is
following their own style, then this makes it

00:01:40.663 --> 00:01:46.623
exceedingly difficult and time-consuming for other
developers within the team to interpret the code.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:54.773
It is crucial for a coding team to have proper standards
to maintain quality, so any developer in the team can

00:01:54.813 --> 00:02:01.323
understand any part of the code that another developer
has written and change or update it independently.

00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:08.783
It can help new developers joining the team giving them
clear guidelines and enabling them to fit in more easily.

00:02:09.393 --> 00:02:15.433
It would reduce the development cost and time as
the code could be reused and create better team

00:02:15.443 --> 00:02:18.673
integration as the code would be consistent.

